You sysgenned an RSX-11S image using its big brother 11-M. The
to-be-target image lived on disk on the 11-M system. You then created
partitions and installed tasks on the target image with VMR which sorta
meant Virtual MCR. It was a poor relation of MCR, 11-M's 'shell' for
want of a better name. VMR worked on the 11-S image pretending it was
already in the target's memory.

...then SAV/WB took an argument for the target volume, in our case a
TU58, which when written was transferred to the target hardware which
was booted from the TU58. The image /finally/ (remember the free
management bar?) got control transferred to it and it started driving
the plant or whatever.

