They are long gone. Besides the fun part was done in Macro-11. Teco
macros let the plant engineers lay out the mimic panels on the display
however they wanted it to look like the real plant.

The AED 512 had 8 bits per pixel (512*512) but that was an index into
an array of 24 bit colours. So you wrote tiger stripes of four 8 bit
colours for the pipe contents (teco created the Macro-11 source for
that), then updated four of the 24 bit values in the table to reflect
temperature, then 'rotated' the four among themselves at a rate
proportional to the flow, getting the effect of all those light bulbs
outside 50's movie houses.

